A||||13||newfie||V8 vs V6 turbo||||5||
Z||000000||newfie||04-25-2000||10:51 AM||||I've been doing some research around the net before I purchase my first Fiero.  I plan on making the car highly modified and want performance.  I see some people have V8 kits.  I find it hard to believe that the Fiero can handle a V8.  And the performance handling must be greatly affected.  I've seen some people who have done turbochargers on the 2.8 and get some impressive numbers.  Anyway I would appreciate any comments on which is better? Where are some good sites for information on this subject? ||||reg||5
Z||000001||GT Bastard||04-25-2000||12:00 PM||||Here's a home-built jobbie.<BR> <A HREF="http://members.xoom.com/dlagrua/turbo.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://members.xoom.com/dlagrua/turbo.htm</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000002||Old Lar||04-25-2000||12:05 PM||||Check out V8 Archies web site.  You can put just about any engine in any car, the limiting factor is $$$$.<P>With some special fabrication, you can do logitudinal engines into a Fiero.  It all depends on your talent or your wallet.<P>If you have to ask, you can't afford it.||||reg||10
Z||000003||1FST2M6||04-25-2000||01:44 PM||||neither .. try Nitrous... I'm going the Positive displacement supercharger... I had a centrifugal unit that was never out of the box but my lack of research stuck me with it... with help from a forum member he lead me in the right direction... I've since swaped it for a Magnusen M90. I'm looking for GOBS of low end torque and this is where i think i'll get the numbers i want... the S/C unit will replace the A/C compressor and be piped in like the Saleen S/C kits for the S281...  with nitrous i had these numbers at the wheel...   <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/2m6dyno.jpg" TARGET=_blank>www.fiero.nl/uploads/2m6dyno.jpg</A>  the lower #s are off the bottle the higher are on it.. add 27.5% for drive line loss for flywheel numbers. (27.5% was given to me by local Pontiac Dealer... Carl Black Pontiac Kennesaw, Ga.) ||||reg||1
Z||000004||Alex4mula||04-25-2000||03:00 PM||||I was thinking last night about the SC idea and how to keep the A/C (too hot in FL). I thought that maybe if you relocate the battery you may have some space there to squeeze a blower. I didn't look into the engine compartment to verify if things would align. Just an idea..||||reg||3
Z||000005||1FST2M6||04-25-2000||04:36 PM||||we have also thought about that.. but then realized the reverse rotation issue... facing the wrong direction the S/C would spin backwards...  forcing the air out the intake of the power head. not sure how that would work... there is enought room.. just not sure about the rotation issue. I'm in Atlanta.. I'm just gunna have to suck it up and deal with it... Fortunatly my Alfa 164 has working A/C and comfy leather seats... for those insanly high temp days...||||reg||1
Z||000006||olympic||04-25-2000||05:22 PM||||   You can get a reverse rotation blower for some applications.  I remember seeing one on a SHO Taurus.  The blower was mounted on the opposite end of the motor as the pulleys and was turned by a long shaft .||||reg||1
Z||000007||Dan86gt3.8||04-25-2000||09:17 PM||||newfie, have you checked into 3.8's and tdc 3.4 engines or a northstar?  thoose can be 300+ ventures, while still keeping the wieght down.  Try <A HREF="http://www.americancustomeng.com," TARGET=_blank>www.americancustomeng.com,</A>  and chris west (forgot his site).  As y name suggests I have a 3.8 (no forced induction <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif">, yet).  Anywho just letting you know there is more than building the 2l8, and shoehorning a small block  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000008||Phil||04-25-2000||10:57 PM||||For 3800s and v8s and the like along with suspension and brake upgrades which should be part of a performance swap Be ready to spend $10,000 by the time you're through. Tain't cheap||||reg||1
Z||000009||RobertVol||04-25-2000||11:25 PM||||With my conversion I spent about $4,600.00.I got the engine from my friend at a Buick Dealership for about 1,400.00 dollars. and then rest on the coversion. I had it done. did not do the install but did no major suspension mods or tranny mods other than clutch changes which were really by choice not necessity. But I love it! mines is the 3.8 GEN II Sc engine. Smooth acceleration and great reliablity....the engine was replacement for a Riveria but was not used. It was a great deal But add the additional cost for new engine in most cases and you are still looking at only total ..7,500 - 8,000.00 and thats paying someone to do it! You could always find a USED engine and install locally if you can find someone in the area or as Dan said ACE ...  ||||reg||1
Z||000010||stimpy||04-25-2000||11:33 PM||||In the interest of equal time for the opposing candidate, I gotta throw in the word for Chris West. ACE may have it when it comes to the kit, but when it comes down to the craftmanship of doing a PRIMO install, West Coast Fiero is the place to go.<P>Again, just an opinion. One shared by others, but an opinion nonetheless.||||reg||1
Z||000011||RobertVol||04-25-2000||11:55 PM||||Agreed Stimp......I MUST be one of the few that got good results from ACE. Mine was excellent!!!! but I havnt found anyone else as happy at all. I must have got lucky ||||reg||1
Z||000012||WKDFIRO||04-26-2000||04:50 AM|||| I have been very lucky to have driven several different Fieros of some gracious members in our club. An 88 GT V8, 88 GT Air to Water Intercooled Turbo Automatic and an 86 SE 3.4 DOHC of Chris West. It was very nicely done and should not be missed should you have a chance to see it! I drove a Zumalt kit years ago and it was amazing. A 91 Vette iron block with aluminum heads in an 88 GT 5 speed built by Fiero Motorsports was probably the most ultimate Fiero that I had the good fortune to drive. Handling? If you have a V8 it goes without saying that at the very least urethane bushings all the way through and an upgrade in brakes are a must. The chassis handled the V8 like it was built to have one, (I think the engineers secretly did this on purpose!) I would prefer the brutality of a small block low end torque to a sneaky quick, no break in the boost fast, 3.4 pushrod turbo automatic. Thats my opinions based on my experience.||||reg||14
Z||000013||BENGAL4||04-26-2000||10:53 AM||||Newfie,<P>Since your from Canada, I think you should contact The Fiero Shop/Race Tech Automotive in Toronto. They are doing my 3800 II S/C swap right now. Joe Trentadue can answer all of your questions.  <A HREF="http://www.thefieroshop.com" TARGET=_blank>www.thefieroshop.com</A> <P>Good Luck||||reg||14
